{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms","id":1548401,"title":"How do the Traditions view opposing Paradigms","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-02-11T09:03:43-08:00","id":1548401,"title":"How do the Traditions view opposing Paradigms","content":"Something I've wondered, but can't seem to find a definite answer, but how do mages with opposing Paradigms view/rationalize each other's magick. (ex. Etherite and Verbena, or Tradition and Technocracy). Is it that they attempt to rationalize what other mages do under their worldview (&quot;Oh it's not the blood sacrifice that makes it work, it's the application of Ether blah blah blah...&quot;) or do the mages understand that belief is what fuels their magic, and therefore no one is entirely 'wrong' in their practice. Or is neither correct and I'm missing something?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28584-matter911","name":"Matter911","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":1,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-11T09:30:15-08:00","id":1548402,"title":"","content":"I would think that most Tradition mages lean more toward the second theory you mentioned.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-11T14:06:38-08:00","id":1548407,"title":"","content":"Broadly speaking most mages know that any belief system can empower magic... it's kinda indisputable the second they start running into each other as their magic works despite all of those differing beliefs.<br /> <br /> One of the primary conflicts of the setting is what each group does with that knowledge. The Technocracy is essentially built on the fear of this truth as clearly horrible paradigms such as those held by the Nephandi work, and thus was founded to try to eliminate or at least mitigate this as much as possible to get rid of the &quot;bad&quot; paradigms even if it means sacrificing potentially neutral or &quot;good&quot; paradigms along the way. So the Technocracy ranks paradigms based on compatibility with their organizational goals, and then actively suppresses ones that don't make the cut; inherently acknowledging that those paradigms work on some level, but not accept that they have to be allowed to work. The Traditions, on the other hand, organized in response to being the proverbial babies the pre-Technocracy was trying to toss out with the bathwater (though some of them where pretty bathwater-y as it was), and as such while the proto-Traditions were frequently not such, the Traditions themselves took the universalist approach of paradigm just being the belief system that empower magic. So an Etherite and a Verbena might struggle to get their magic to line up enough to work together directly on an effect, but philosophically they acknowledge that their mutually exclusive approaches to magic aren't inherently better or worse. That said, the Traditions don't hold all paradigms as equal, but it's judged much more in terms of things like the Tolerance Paradox where they can't abide the Technocracy or Nephandi who want to make everything one way.<br /> <br /> The Crafts and Disparates tend to be somewhere in the middle (on a spectrum since ex-Traditions and ex-Conventions both exist in this category with some of the associated philosophic baggage). Most could be summed up as a &quot;first among equals,&quot; stance where acknowledge others as having valid paradigms enough that actively opposing other mages on principle alone (besides the Nephandi for the Disparates) isn't really justifiable, but use some form of justification to claim their way of doing things is still better than everyone else's (thus don't side with either the Technocracy's desire for one paradigm to rule them all, or the Traditions' desire to get everyone to play nicely together by giving up on some of their sense of uniqueness).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-13T13:00:01-08:00","id":1548479,"title":"","content":"A cleric in Dungeons and Dragons uses the favor of the gods to make a fireball. A wizard uses their understanding of arcane theory to make a fireball. A druid channels the power of nature to do that, an alchemist makes a bomb, and a sorcerer uses their inner power. There's no reason mages can't view what they're doing the same way. Just because they're producing similar effects doesn't mean they're actually doing the same thing. Not all mages think belief is that important, and for those that don't &quot;I'm doing etheric science, they're doing blood magic&quot; is a perfectly workable paradigm.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-14T23:33:50-08:00","id":1548509,"title":"","content":"Humans are remarkably adept at rationalizing new information to salvage their preexisting beliefs. It's an ability written into our DNA, and it generally works from a survival perspective. (We only pay attention to it when it starts causing problems.)<br /> <br /> Mages <i>could</i> accept that the post-modern theory that Magick and Reality are affect by Belief unto itself. Some, especially in the Traditions, do so as a matter of practicality or polite conduct with their allies. But that kind of thinking makes a Mage confront that their own belief system might be <i>wrong</i>, or at least only <i>conditionally right</i>. That way lies Cognitive Dissonance, and fuck that noise. It's easier in some ways, rather, to make allowances for Magick outside one's own methods, that still keep their personal beliefs intact. That the other Mage CAN do what they do, but either because it's allowed within the first Mage's Paradigm, or because the first Mage's methods are &quot;better&quot; or &quot;purer&quot;.<br /> <br /> A Mage who believes Magick comes from God or the Spirits can easily say that God/Spirits allow their Arts to work, for reasons known only to them. And despite that the non-believer doesn't give God/Spirits the proper respect. The greater powers decided the other Mage's needs or actions were worth it. (Or, conversely, that a competing entity is acting on their behalf, like a rival spirit or &quot;The Devil&quot;).<br /> <br /> A Mage who believes that the world obeys some kind of laws (whatever those laws may be, from Scientific to Arcane to Fey) understands that anything could potentially exploit them. (THEIR methods are clearly just superior, obviously, as they have a clearer understanding of it).<br /> <br /> <br /> Setting aside Conditioning to train personnel to engage in &quot;Crimestop&quot; against &quot;Wrongthink&quot;, the Technocracy may have a sliding scale of excuses given to its agents for why mystics can do what they do. The lower levels (Extraordinary Citizens and low-level Enlightened) are convinced to view Traditionalists and Crafts as outright charlatans engaged in parlor tricks, or else using some misunderstood or cleverly hidden artifice.<br /> <br /> As an agent's level of security clearance grows, they may be taught that mystics employ very ancient, very unreliable/unstable psychic disciplines. That their Enlightenment &quot;works&quot;, but puts the entire weight of channeling Scientific principles on the fragile minds of its throwback practitioners. A practice that inevitably leads to psychosis, delusion, psychosomatic injury, or unintentional manifestations of the superstitionist's cultural fears into the physical world. (Read: Paradox). Ergo, it's clearly preferable for Enlightened personnel to externalize their Procedures through vetted technology that, when it fails, fails reliably and safely (usually by breaking the hypertech tool). Agents should leave Psychic effects to the mystics, and to trained, sanctioned, regulated Union specialists who understand the risks.<br /> <br /> It's only when the Union rep achieves a high enough threshold of clearance, and when their loyalty is unquestionable (or when they're judged as &quot;needs to know&quot;), will the Union come clean about the nature of Magick. That Belief itself is a necessary component. By this time, of course, the agent will be trusted to understand, fully, WHY the Technocratic Union MUST control the Consensus. Why their vision of Reality is a necessary one, for the good of the Sleepers and the world.<br /> <br /> <br /> Finally, it's worth realizing that, even if a Mage has a belief over why their methods work, that does preclude them accepting the world is a big place. Mysterious and mercurial. That there is more between heaven and earth than is dreamt of in their philosophy. The methods of others doesn't invalidate one's own, much less one's Paradigm. It just means there's more going on behind the scenes. Some may endeavor to fill those gaps, reasoning that other Mages may, if imperfectly, understand something they don't. Alternatively, the Mage may simply shrug, and accept such mysteries as unavoidable or unsolvable.<br /> <br /> Does the mad scientist NEED to know why the witch's broom can fly? Does the Chorister NEED to know why the mad scientist's raygun works? Or can they trust that they do, and leave well enough alone?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-16T23:45:40-08:00","id":1548544,"title":"","content":"Another issue is how broad a mage considers her <i>own </i>Focus to be. There's a difference between &quot;This is how the world works&quot; and &quot;this is how my Magick works&quot;. Both provide a framework for how to work your own Effects; but the former also implies that <i>everything </i>needs to be explainable according to your Focus, while the latter leaves room for the mage to admit that she doesn't have all the answers, and that other kinds of Magick reside in the gaps. <br /> <br /> Early editions of the game lean heavily toward the former, while more recent editions allow for either, depending on the mage.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-29T08:03:49-07:00","id":1550481,"title":"","content":"I have long said that if you do not believe belief is at the root of your magic, you are a Hedge Mage not Awakened.<br /> <br /> Even the Technocracy in my games knows that their magic comes from Belief (coached in scientific terms) and that's partially what makes their desire to eradicate other mages so horrifying.<br /> <br /> They know it's no different but they view it as an existential threat to have OTHER people know it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-29T10:15:56-07:00","id":1550484,"title":"","content":"To paraphrase Terry Pratchett:<br /> <br /> &quot;The <s>astro-philosophers of Krull</s> Traditions once succeeded in proving conclusively that all <s>places</s> paradigms are one <s>place</s> paradigm and that the <s>distance</s> difference between them is an illusion, and this news was an embarrassment to all thinking <s>philosophers</s> mages because it did not explain, among other things, <s>signposts</s> spells. After years of wrangling, the whole thing was then turned over to Lyn Tin Wheedle, arguably the Disc\u2019s greatest philosopher, who after some thought proclaimed that although it was indeed true that all <s>places</s> paradigms were one <s>place</s> paradigm, that <s>place</s> paradigm was <i>very large.</i>&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-29T16:01:03-07:00","id":1550492,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1550481#post1550481\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have long said that if you do not believe belief is at the root of your magic, you are a Hedge Mage not Awakened.<br /> <br /> Even the Technocracy in my games knows that their magic comes from Belief (coached in scientific terms) and that's partially what makes their desire to eradicate other mages so horrifying.<br /> <br /> They know it's no different but they view it as an existential threat to have OTHER people know it.</div> </div> </div> </div><b>Technocracy Reloaded</b> states that while they're aware that what they do alters reality, they absolutely <i>do not</i> accept that what they do is the same as what the Traditions do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-29T17:45:19-07:00","id":1550494,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1550492#post1550492\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Technocracy Reloaded</b> states that while they're aware that what they do alters reality, they absolutely <i>do not</i> accept that what they do is the same as what the Traditions do.</div> </div> </div> </div>I felt like that really made the Technocracy look stupid.<br /> <br /> 1. It means that they have no ability to actually do science because scientific experimentation would reveal that, yes, they are. So they're just deluded.<br /> <br /> 2. It means they fundamentally don't understand a central tenant of reality. So they can hardly call themselves Awakened or Enlightened.<br /> <br /> 3. It means the Traditions are massively more intelligent than them.<br /> <br /> 4. If Awakening doesn't show you how reality works then what IS Awakening?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-29T18:37:19-07:00","id":1550497,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1550494#post1550494\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I felt like that really made the Technocracy look stupid.<br /> <br /> 1. It means that they have no ability to actually do science because scientific experimentation would reveal that, yes, they are. So they're just deluded.<br /> <br /> 2. It means they fundamentally don't understand a central tenant of reality. So they can hardly call themselves Awakened or Enlightened.</div> </div> </div> </div>Both of these assume that the Traditions are right. <br /> <br /> Remember: I said that the Technocracy <i>does</i> realize that what they do changes reality. What they <i>disagree on </i>is that what they do and what the Traditions do are the same thing. Yes, the Traditions <i>also</i> have a way of changing Reality; but it's not the <i>same</i> way. And they can point to at least two pieces of evidence supporting that: first, that Technocrats can't just force something to happen by will alone (what M20 calls &quot;Working Without Focus&quot;); and second, that the Traditions cannot make Talismans as easily as Technocrats can make Devices: the latter have a way of getting around the need to spend personal Quintessence on the effort, nor to invest a dot of Willpower to conclude the process. These might seem like minor nitpicks; but they point to a more profound distinction between Magick and Enlightened Science: the former is Willworking; the latter, not so much. <br /> <br /> And finally, there's the fact that Devices have the Coincidental edge, and not <i>just</i> because of Technocratic propaganda: Devices are more likely to be Coincidental because Enlightened Science tends to extrapolate from the Earthly Foundations, while Magick is more likely to just try to override said Foundations. <br /> <br /> I'm fine with that distinction, as it gives the Technocracy a way out of the &quot;liars or dupes&quot; box they're stuck in if you <i>do</i> assume that the Traditions are objectively right \u2014 as opposed to &quot;we're all doing the same thing&quot; being something that the <i>Traditions</i> believe (and have reason to promote, as it's what holds the Traditions together), but which might not actually be true. <br /> <br /> Which gets back to what I said in my first post in this thread: there's a difference between &quot;this is how what I do works&quot; and &quot;this is how the world works&quot;. The former doesn't make any grand claims about Life, the Universe, and Everything, and so doesn't have to explain as much: the Technocrat who isn't trying to insist that Science explains literally <i>everything</i> doesn't have to provide Scientific explanations for how a Hermetic High Ritual works; he's free to just say &quot;that's not Science; it's dangerous Superstitionism, and you'll regret it if you get involved with it,&quot; <i>without</i> having to say &quot;Magick isn't real&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-30T03:38:02-07:00","id":1550504,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1550497#post1550497\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Both of these assume that the Traditions are right.<br /> <br /> Remember: I said that the Technocracy <i>does</i> realize that what they do changes reality. What they <i>disagree on </i>is that what they do and what the Traditions do are the same thing. Yes, the Traditions <i>also</i> have a way of changing Reality; but it's not the <i>same</i> way. And they can point to at least two pieces of evidence supporting that: first, that Technocrats can't just force something to happen by will alone (what M20 calls &quot;Working Without Focus&quot;); and second, that the Traditions cannot make Talismans as easily as Technocrats can make Devices: the latter have a way of getting around the need to spend personal Quintessence on the effort, nor to invest a dot of Willpower to conclude the process. These might seem like minor nitpicks; but they point to a more profound distinction between Magick and Enlightened Science: the former is Willworking; the latter, not so much.<br /> <br /> And finally, there's the fact that Devices have the Coincidental edge, and not <i>just</i> because of Technocratic propaganda: Devices are more likely to be Coincidental because Enlightened Science tends to extrapolate from the Earthly Foundations, while Magick is more likely to just try to override said Foundations.<br /> <br /> I'm fine with that distinction, as it gives the Technocracy a way out of the &quot;liars or dupes&quot; box they're stuck in if you <i>do</i> assume that the Traditions are objectively right \u2014 as opposed to &quot;we're all doing the same thing&quot; being something that the <i>Traditions</i> believe (and have reason to promote, as it's what holds the Traditions together), but which might not actually be true.<br /> <br /> Which gets back to what I said in my first post in this thread: there's a difference between &quot;this is how what I do works&quot; and &quot;this is how the world works&quot;. The former doesn't make any grand claims about Life, the Universe, and Everything, and so doesn't have to explain as much: the Technocrat who isn't trying to insist that Science explains literally <i>everything</i> doesn't have to provide Scientific explanations for how a Hermetic High Ritual works; he's free to just say &quot;that's not Science; it's dangerous Superstitionism, and you'll regret it if you get involved with it,&quot; <i>without</i> having to say &quot;Magick isn't real&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean doesn't just reverse it?<br /> <br /> Now the Union is objectively right that they don't do the same thing.<br /> <br /> And I feel it was much better when BOTH were objectively right that they know they're doing the same thing but think that it is worth killing over the Foci. Basically, the Ascension War was something similar to the 40 Years War in that the conflict is between something that only makes sense to people who agree about 90% of the same things but those 10% things that people outside of mages couldn't care less about are worth killing over.<br /> <br /> I,e. &quot;Do magick with technology&quot; or &quot;do magick with anything.&quot;<br /> <br /> YMMV of course.<br /> <br /> But I do think Revised wasn't WRONG that the Ascension War was inherently silly. It's just that it stopped being silly on a personal level since most people weren't fighting it over the fundamental philosophical principle after a time but because they'd lost tons of friends to it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-30T05:58:37-07:00","id":1550511,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1550504#post1550504\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mean doesn't just reverse it?<br /> <br /> Now the Union is objectively right that they don't do the same thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>No. Just because the Traditions aren't objectively right, it doesn't follow that they're objectively <i>wrong</i>. They <i>could</i> be right; but there's no way to know. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-02T14:51:03-07:00","id":1550609,"title":"","content":"Looking back at Ascension, I really feel like the Technocracy as an organization was rather silly, which is kind of unfortunate, as I really liked the aesthetic that they presented. It just feels like they suffered from the sort of concretization that a lot of things in Ascension suffered from, especially as the metaplot continued. On one hand its cool to have the MIB shadow organization with cool tech and weird paranormal research; on the other hand, the Technocracy as written either comes across as deluded or weirdly obsessed with one way of presenting their magic and influencing the Consensus, and I think that their name itself really does a lot of the legwork there. The ideological drive against the Traditions very much feels like a forced &quot;old magic versus soulless science&quot; thing that is both dated and just not very philosophically compelling. And really, one can apply to that reasoning to a lot of things in the setting. Why do the Traditions have to be organizations? Why does your Tradition/Craft/Convention/etc. have to be so important when every Awakened is an enlightened mystic who probably has some inanely complicated and hyper-specific paradigm of their own that could hardly fall within the reach of a splat book? Why does the Order of Hermes has a GD-esque grade system when that kind of thing would probably just be for their hedge mages? I think that Ascension especially suffered from the way in which the splats were essentially forced onto them, which is kind of funny given how mechanically their splats have the least impact (e.g., Focus Sphere, and even then you can just be an Orphan and pick your own). Honestly I think that the Technocracy could have really worked as an organization focused on refining magic into its basic, most unadorned principles in an early British chaos magic sort of way. You could just add on skepticism of the religious narratives of the Traditions (e.g., questioning just how much mystical experiences like the Awakening should be modelled with religious and magical systems that are present in the culture that one has grown up in). Then you could make them have advanced mundane tech just for the cool factor. Throw in a bit of breakaway civilizations or something for external conflict and you're good.<br /> <br /> I do agree with Phipps in that the Awakening should confer some sort of privileged knowledge about the nature of reality in order for it to be significant narratively. As presented, the Technocracy does come across like something out of a comic book, even though they acknowledge the Tradition mages can manipulate reality. Ascension as a whole could really benefit from boiling down the game to its basic premise. If someone wants to play VTM they probably want to play Dracula or Count Orlocke. They can play MTA if they want to be Jesus or Mahavira or something. At that point, having an excessively calcified setting with a commitment to a 1990s interpretation of the relationship between magic and science just gets in the way. When Ascension was first published a lot of its themes might have been largely invisible in society, but now the basic postmodern magic &quot;thing&quot; that Ascension had is relatively common in culture. A lot of STs and players could come up with good ideas for campaigns and the like just from reading various books.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude0e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-02T22:52:38-07:00","id":1550618,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"28352\" data-vbnamecard=\"28352\">Verlan</a> has a lot of good points.<br /> <br /> I think the heart of the matter is that every RPG needs a good antagonist that is something that gives the player characters something to fight against but also something that underlines the thematic ideas of the game. V:TM has the War of Ages and War of the Sabbat that provides a question of the corrupting effects of immortality as well as the Humanity issue. Werewolf has the conflict with the Wyrm but also the Garou constantly at each other's throats.<br /> <br /> The issue with the Technocracy vs. the Traditions is that the early Technocracy wants to control all of human advancement and shove it into a narrow box. Maybe even remove anyone Awakening but the way they want. But the latter Technocracy does want to do the Mass Ascension the same way as the Traditions. So it becomes a question of, &quot;why are they fighting at all?&quot;<br /> <br /> To go with the Seers of Thrones example, the conflict is actually pretty thematic of people who want to escape the prison of the Exarch's world versus the people who want to keep everyone locked up. You need the anti-Ascension people in Mage and they just don't exist. The Nephandi exist but they're Oblivion rather than Stasis.<br /> <br /> It makes me wonder if they did a Mage 5 (I still have my doubts), they should probably make a Technocracy/Sons of Ether/VA group that is reformed, a Traditions, a Disparate Alliance-esque group and then a Seers-esque group alongside the Nephandi.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-03T06:21:53-07:00","id":1550622,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The issue with the Technocracy vs. the Traditions is that the early Technocracy wants to control all of human advancement and shove it into a narrow box. Maybe even remove anyone Awakening but the way they want. But the latter Technocracy does want to do the Mass Ascension the same way as the Traditions. So it becomes a question of, &quot;why are they fighting at all?&quot; </div> </div> </div>That's not actually true, though. Even in MRev, &quot;the Trap&quot; I mentioned before, the one that tends to capture intellectuals and that the Technocracy is emblematic of? It involves being so in love with the elegance of a model of what Reality is supposed to be like that when the model and Reality clash, the intellectual favors the model.<br /> <br /> In particular, when humans refuse to behave the way the Technocrat's models predict, the Techie then sets about trying to alter humanity to fit the model, &quot;shov[ing] it in a narrow box.&quot; M20 Technocrats don't have anything that contradicts this, and a big part of what <b>Technocracy Reloaded</b> presents is an attitude of &quot;the Masses have made a royal mess of everything; we need to save them from themselves!&quot;<br /> <br /> As well, while most of the Conventions have a utopian ideal that they're striving for (&quot;Mass Ascension&quot;, if you will), the various visions they have tend to be collective in nature, with humanity being reshaped to facilitate the vision. And &quot;Mass Ascension&quot; is a bit of a misnomer, because the visions involved tend <i>not</i> to include the Masses Ascending, or even Awakening: the Awakened are, by definition, &quot;random elements&quot; that tend to disrupt the Technocracy's models even when the Masses aren't. And everyone who Awakens is a potential Marauder or Nephandus. So pushing for anyone and everyone to Ascend is seen as the height of irresponsibility.<br /> <br /> So all the way through from 1e to M20, the Technocracy does Bad Things; the difference between them and Awakening's Seers of the Throne is that the Technocracy is able to couch its Bad Things in terms of doing it for the Greater Good.<br /> <br /> And, as I pointed out in an early thread that you started here, the Traditions <i>also</i> do Bad Things in service to a Greater Good; just not the <i>same</i> Bad Things that the Technocracy does. Even the Hollow Ones can get caught in a trap where they potentially end up pushing for anyone and everyone to Ascend, regardless of the consequences. (Yes, the Technocracy has a point that indiscriminate Awakenings will lead to more Marauders and Nephandi; and mages who don't take that into consideration are making the opposite error from the one being made by the Technocracy.<br /> <br /> <b>Mage</b> doesn't have <i>any</i> Good Guys, except perhaps the player characters.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":".  Typo 07-05-2025, 09:05 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-03T12:23:41-07:00","id":1550637,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You need the anti-Ascension people in Mage and they just don't exist. </div> </div> </div>What about Voormas? I mean, I think the metaplot pretty clearly implies that he's just the tip of the iceberg and that most of the old masters of the Traditions are in reality anti-ascension, even if they're not conscious of that fact. To be fair, that's not a 'faction', but personally, I think it's a more compelling way of treating anti-ascension as a concept.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude0e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-06T16:40:19-07:00","id":1550773,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1550637#post1550637\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> What about Voormas? I mean, I think the metaplot pretty clearly implies that he's just the tip of the iceberg and that most of the old masters of the Traditions are in reality anti-ascension, even if they're not conscious of that fact. To be fair, that's not a 'faction', but personally, I think it's a more compelling way of treating anti-ascension as a concept.</div> </div> </div> </div>Voormas is a fascinating case of backpedaling because it provides the absolute worst of the Traditions alongside other examples that makes it look like the Traditions aren't any better than the Union.<br /> <br /> Which makes sense if you're doing a Punk game.<br /> <br /> However, Euthanatos tried to make him a crazy outlier and ultimately he became the ultimate enemy of the setting in at least one Mage: The Ascension ending.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-07T04:21:09-07:00","id":1550786,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> However, Euthanatos tried to make him a crazy outlier... </div> </div> </div>I don't really agree. Like, I think his first appearance is <i>Book of Chantries</i>, and he's already explicitly a secret heretic of the Euthanatos in that book.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-07T11:44:25-07:00","id":1550806,"title":"","content":"Old mage made a point that the protagonists are young mages, and old mages are insane and lost - that's the aesop of Porthos: &quot;power it's polish on blunt blades&quot;. Voormas was a chief example in that he doesn't want to die, mere agelessness ain't enough for him because he could still be killed (as an Euthanatoi, he probably killed many &quot;immortals&quot; himself) ... and he's so elite he can actually enforce this vision at a great cost to everyone.<br /> <br /> It's part of the &quot;punk&quot; narrative ... &quot;Technocracy exists because of people like you&quot; sort of deal.<br /> <br /> Ultimately, weter they're right or not, the Technocracy doesn't exist because of some nipicks of how magick &quot;really works&quot;, and if what the Traditions do it's &quot;insufficiently explained science&quot;, or they are different branches of a power to alter reality, but from the idea that magick - all of it - needs to be controlled.<br /> <br /> Their point would be that the Consensus curbs the worst excesses of mad wizards at normal people, and forces monsters that can't &quot;lay low&quot; out of the planet, while their own safer style of magick remains unaffected, and can be shared more freely with sleepers. And the world it's a better place.<br /> <br /> While at some level this it's undeniably true, the Traditions aren't entirely wrong. Not just because they could be able to produce alternative utopies, maybe?, but because this world has it's own unique horrors: Who needs fighting Dragons when you can fight swarms of killer Drones instead? - hence why Tech-guys are so angsty about people being people, and want to &quot;well-intendedly&quot; control everyone. Not that that makes them look any better. <hr /><br /> In my opinion.<br /> <br /> While the old way of seeing this as hippies vs. yuppies it's philosophically poor, our real world has made this whole message about <b>shifting world views, thinking if &quot;our way it's the only way&quot;, and &quot;power&quot; being wasted on evil or blind people</b>, perhaps more important and surely not less.<br /> <br /> *We accept now levels of surveillance and control that were distopic not many years ago - so much so that even &quot;The Man&quot; finds it oddly inconvinient (see how USA &quot;chinese scare&quot; about tiktok &quot;spies&quot; was completely uneffectual and laughed to - maybe if we weren't giving all our data to closer-to-home boogiemans all the time, would've more scared by it?). Yet the world doesn't feel that much safer.<br /> <br /> *Man made weapons of different kinds can destroy humanity more readily than any natural plague or predator ever could (at least since we spread trough the world), but even if we agree that to use them in that capacity it's stupid, and nobody was threatening nobody with them, climate change appears to be in it's way to reset society anyway - we're already past many &quot;points of no return&quot; w/o so much as even globaly agreeing on trying to slow it. &quot;The end of the world it's easier to imagine than the end of Capitalism&quot;, not in a small capacity because the &quot;end of the world&quot; has become rather easy to imagine - I think this *is* the intended use of the phrase, the point it's to imagine a better world not a worse one.<br /> <br /> *Science has provided lives that are both longer and better, but there's a huge problem with *whom* can access these benefits. While plenty of people fight to make these more accessible, there's plenty of people wanting to terminate any sort of political intervention to make healt and stuff more accessible with all sort of elitist arguments about scarcity that ultimately boil down to that if you don't have a lot of money, you probably don't deserve to live that well in the first place.<br /> <br /> All of this with the very enthusiast intervention of Tech-Bros fighting to have even more power than they already have, and spending in think-tanks whose only purpose it's to deny there could be a problem with any of this: Real life ideological warfare to ensure the goals and ideologies of those who pay can't be resisted.<br /> <br /> If the idea that &quot;tech-holds-<u><b>all</b></u>-the-answers&quot; was ever up to <i>any </i>sort of debate...<br /> <br /> And I think modern Mage can, or could, serve as a theater for this debate, questioning what's perceived as normal, and what that means to people with power, under the safer guise of punks fighting for unicorns and cyborgs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-07-2025, 02:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-02T13:04:34-08:00","id":1558083,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1548479#post1548479\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A cleric in Dungeons and Dragons uses the favor of the gods to make a fireball. A wizard uses their understanding of arcane theory to make a fireball. A druid channels the power of nature to do that, an alchemist makes a bomb, and a sorcerer uses their inner power. There's no reason mages can't view what they're doing the same way. Just because they're producing similar effects doesn't mean they're actually doing the same thing. Not all mages think belief is that important, and for those that don't &quot;I'm doing etheric science, they're doing blood magic&quot; is a perfectly workable paradigm.</div> </div> </div> </div><i>&quot;Magic is Magic, just as Water is Water. It differs only in how it is used.&quot;</i> - Solas, Dragon Age Inquisition.<br /> <br /> Verbena believe that the universe around them is inherently magical to some degree, and they simply found a way to tap into it.<br /> <br /> While the Etherites believe that divinity itself can be distilled into a quantifiable substance, which can then be used to power their society.<br /> <br /> It's application may differ, but the core concept isn't too far removed from each other.<br /> <br /> <br /> ---------------------------<br /> <br /> Likewise, both the Akashics and the Euthanatos believe in the cycle of reincarnation. But while the Akashics embrace a zen-like state of noninterference, the Euthanatos prefer to get more involved with the process. Thus leading to the conflict between the two groups.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-26T14:39:11-07:00","id":1562195,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1558083#post1558083\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><i>&quot;Magic is Magic, just as Water is Water. It differs only in how it is used.&quot;</i> - Solas, Dragon Age Inquisition.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yet this is not Dragon Age and Magic definitely isn't that clear cut in Mage. Or WoD.<br /> <br /> A few things have to be clear here:<br /> <br /> 1. Each Paradigm has one (or more) theories on why what they do work. Each also has one (or more) theories on why what the others do work. Full stop. Those theories don't have to agree that they're doing the same thing or that it comes from the same phenomena. It only explains what they see.<br /> <br /> Corolary: their collective history made many of their thesis circulate among the main groups, so there's convergence and cross pollination, but not an actual common ground.<br /> <br /> 2. A lot of the concepts seen in the game are not canonical facts despite narrator presenting as such. Paradox, Paradigm, Consensus, and the very notion of Awakened Mages are actual Hermetic theory, sanitized for neutrality and sponsored as the basic framework of the Traditions.<br /> <br /> Corollary: most Mages actually disagree with something in those theories, some disagree with a lot. Only the Order of Hermes really has a significant belief that they all do the same thing, because this is precisely their Paradigm.<br /> <br /> 3. Mages cannot follow skepticism or scientific method. This would require falsifiability, and by their own nature Mages can't be falsified. Every Mage is capable of producing evidence of their belief, not really proving it, but rendering the question itself moot.<br /> <br /> Corollary: there's no possible distinction between what a Mage is and what they believe they are. If a Mage believes to be a shaman, no test will ever prove they're actually a Mage. The Traditions are united by historical recognition and a common cause, not by the belief they are the same thing, even if mist agree this belief is plausible.<br /> <br /> 4. Similarity is layered. Believing that they all change reality, or they all channel the One, is not believing their distinctions are just window dressing. They may believe in the methods and that different methods require different kinds of people. What it takes to do Magic as one Tradition isn't the same to do it as another.<br /> <br /> Corollary: the distinction, not the similarity, is objectively correct. If each faction is actually a completely independent phenomena, they will still work exactly as they do, so the difference is real. And if they're indeed Willworkers, their reality-shaping beliefs cause the distinctions to exist, so the difference is real.<br /> <br /> 5. High Caps Magic is but one type of magic on the setting. Sorcerers are real, kinfolk ritualists are real, Gifts are real, Thaumaturgy is real, Hekau is real. The difference has observable precedents. Any Mage is indistinguishable from an extremely competent practitioner of such &quot;lesser magics&quot;.<br /> <br /> Corollary: Magick is one theory in a world with demonstrable alternative explanations. The rules may, indeed, be lying to you without breaking canon.<br /> <br /> 6. Paradox changes manifestation to match a Paradigm. Avatars do the same. And Seekings. Those actually contradict standard theories exactly in the few evidences of commonality.<br /> <br /> Corollary: the very notion of Magick can only exist while making itself impossible to confirm, as it has to accommodate contradictions to itself at every turn. The one thing that unifies all Mages as a splat is unknowable if correct.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-26T16:30:22-07:00","id":1562205,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1562195#post1562195\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> 5. High Caps Magic is but one type of magic on the setting. Sorcerers are real, kinfolk ritualists are real, Gifts are real, Thaumaturgy is real, Hekau is real. The difference has observable precedents. Any Mage is indistinguishable from an extremely competent practitioner of such &quot;lesser magics&quot;.<br /> <br /> Corollary: Magick is one theory in a world with demonstrable alternative explanations. The rules may, indeed, be lying to you without breaking canon.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> But wouldn't this imply that Sorcerers lack creativity and imagination? <br /> <br /> Unlike Mages, a Sorcerer can not create new spells. They can only work with whatever tools are provided to them. Contrasted by Mages, who have the capacity to make their own tools, as well as dictate the rules behind how / why the rituals are able to function.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\u2615","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-26T16:50:49-07:00","id":1562212,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1562205#post1562205\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But wouldn't this imply that Sorcerers lack creativity and imagination?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes. That's the point.<br /> <br /> This is the subtext of Mage. What defines them for themselves isn't that they do another kind of magic, but that they are geniuses at what they do.<br /> <br /> For most of History that's all there was for it. Mages weren't seen as ontologically different from other practitioners of whatever they did, but as clearly better, way more insightful.<br /> <br /> So by the time of Plato it was clear for magical societies that Mages exist, but here &quot;Mages&quot; are understood just as really talented members that got to invent stuff.<br /> <br /> One can say it's not a lack of creativity per se, because Sorcerers don't have a problem with coming up with the idea, but with a means to make it work. But poteyto, potahto, same result and effect, that's why superiority is so deeply ingrained in their culture.<br /> <br /> All the ruckus about everyone being the same thing, and maybe not as smarter than peers as they thought, started by get real momentum by the 8th century. And it was linked with a rise in Platonism that endorses the concept of the &quot;Philosopher-King&quot;. Mage politics at the time was nice and wholesome...<br /> <br /> But yeah, metaphysically we actually only know for sure that Mages are really talented at their Paradigms. Everything else is actually just an educated guess at best, and Sorcerers may be just &quot;dumb&quot; Mages.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-27T01:03:37-07:00","id":1562219,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1562205#post1562205\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Unlike Mages, a Sorcerer can not create new spells. They can only work with whatever tools are provided to them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, Sorcerers <i>can</i> create new spells. They can't do so on the fly, as it takes months of research; but they can still do it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-01T15:36:35-07:00","id":1562446,"title":"","content":"Going off of the talk of the relationship between mages and sorcerers, I don\u2019t know how much this corresponds to canon but I\u2019ve always liked the idea that most forms of sorcery are downstream from Mages.<br /> <br /> Every Mage is at least implicitly a schism or sect in embryo, with the potential to become the pioneer and founder of a new movement or understanding of how the universe works. When they do this, you end up with sorcerers coming downstream of them who imitate the practices they established, albeit usually with less dramatic results and more difficulty.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-01T16:21:32-07:00","id":1562448,"title":"","content":"The only issue is the sorcerers don't have to be downstream of mages specifically. That's probably true of most modern sorcerers that aren't adopting older schools of thought, but we have plenty examples of sorcerers orienting their arts &quot;downstream&quot; of other supernatural practices. The obvious example is kinfolk numina users that practice arts aligned with Gaian philosophy and metaphysics.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-01T21:16:16-07:00","id":1562455,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1562446#post1562446\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Going off of the talk of the relationship between mages and sorcerers, I don\u2019t know how much this corresponds to canon but I\u2019ve always liked the idea that most forms of sorcery are downstream from Mages.</div> </div> </div> </div>It doesn't contradict canon in Mage, but isn't supported either, and for themselves reasons Heavy Arms said it does contradict canon in other lines.<br /> <br /> Part of the problem is a chicken-egg situation: Mages are schismatic by nature, but they start at some point. So there's always a previous tradition, and that brings the question: even if the Mage has followers, are their magics more derived from the Mage or from the Sorcerers that preceded them?<br /> <br /> Sorcerers also make their own schisms and sects, they don't need Mages for this. How many schisms are really inspired by Mages?<br /> <br /> What about the actual metaphysics of reality? Consensus or no Consensus, the world has rules and supernatural powers come from this. The rules are not defined by Mages no matter how much they influence culture. How many Sorcerers are independent discoverers of such rules?<br /> <br /> Those all depend a lot on how much you want Mages to be prevalent and influential in the setting. And, of course, your definition/criteria for &quot;downstream&quot;. I don't particularly like this idea because I feel it limits what I can put in my chronicles as a contrast to Mages. But that's a me thing, obviously. I nonetheless prefer a tone of &quot;There are more things in Heaven and Earth than are dreamt of in your philosophy&quot; for my games.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-02T08:34:38-07:00","id":1562489,"title":"","content":"I would say that if you like the idea that all sorcery is downstream from mages, you can easily implement that in your games by focusing on mages as individuals rather than mages as represented by groups. Perhaps the single most genius move that M20 made was to separate Focus from allegiance: you no longer have to play an Etherite to play a mad scientist, or a Verbena to play a witch; you need only select Weird Science or Witchcraft as your Practice. And since, as if <b>M20 Sorcerer</b>, sorcery <i>also </i>uses those same Paradigms, Practices, and Instruments, sorcery is effectively \u201cdownstream\u201d from Awakened Magick. <br /> <br /> As for the thaumaturges featured in <b>Hunters Hunted 2</b>, the animists featured in <b>Kinfolk: Unsung Heroes</b>, and mediums featured in <b>Ghost Hunters</b>: while they <i>are</i> based in the metaphysics of <b>Vampire</b>, <b>Werewolf</b>, and <b>Wraith</b>, respectively, and they <i>don't</i> import the Focus system from <b>Mage</b>, that Focus system still allows you to make mages who are compatible with those other gamelines' metaphysics. And those thaumaturges, animists, and mediums can be considered to be \u201cdownstream\u201d from those crossover-friendly mages, if you want to go there. <br /> <br /> But to reinforce <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a>' point, it's at least as valid to say that <b>HH2</b> thaumaturges derive their power from <b>Vampire</b> metaphysics, that <b>KUH</b> animists derive their power from <b>Werewolf</b> metaphysics, that <b>M20 Sorcerer</b>s derive their power from <b>Mage</b> metaphysics, and that <b>GH</b> mediums derive their power from <b>Wraith</b> metaphysics. And since <b>Vampire</b>, <b>Werewolf</b>, and <b>Wraith</b> metaphysics don't involve Mythic Threads, the associated thaumaturges, animists, and mediums wouldn't be the result of tapping into Mythic Threads; they'd be ordinary mortals who tap into the metaphysics of the other gamelines. <br /> <br /> <hr /><br /> <br /> Personally, I'd like to see more diversity in the gameline-adjacent \u201cmortals with supernatural power\u201d idea. <b>Changeling</b> handles it through a system whereby some mortals have an ability to tap into the gameline's Arts and Realms; the \u201cYear of the Ally\u201d supplement for <b>Wraith</b> (<b>The Quick and the Dead</b>) had a Merits-and-Flaws based system for defining mediums; and the \u201cYear of the Ally\u201d supplement for <b>Vampire</b> (<b>Ghouls</b>) had a system built on ghouls being able to learn and use a limited selection of Disciplines. I wouldn't mind expanding on one or both of those concepts instead of the Hedge Paths system: allow mortals with the right supernatural Merits to learn and use a subset of Thaumaturgy or one of the other \u201cBlood Sorceries\u201d that vampires can access; allow appropriate mortals to learn and perform some <b>Werewolf</b> Rites; allow something downstream from <b>Mage</b> by giving some mortals the ability to learn and perform Focus-appropriate Rotes; and allow some appropriate mortals to learn and use an appropriate subset of <b>Wraith</b>'s Arcanoi. Likewise with <b>Mummy</b>'s Hekau and <b>Demon</b>'s Lores. (<b>Hunter</b> has Bystanders; but almost by definition, they don't and can't have the Edges that the Imbued have. Just Supernatural Merits that give lesser versions of the innate powers that every Imbued gets.)<br /> <br /> Supplement this by using Supernatural Merits and Flaws to represent mortal-specific powers and conditions related to that gameline's metaphysics: one or two \u201cgateways\u201d that define what it takes for mortals to access the gameline's usual powers, and a few other traits that define other things that the mortal can do or has to cope with that the gameline's usual protagonists don't. (See <b>Changeling</b>'s system, particularly the Bestowments that the Enchanted can acquire, for an example that could be treated as gameline-specific mortal-only Supernatural Merits; and see the aforementioned <b>The Quick and the Dead</b> for a similar system that actually manifests as Merits and Flaws.) <br /> <br /> If this concept were to get implemented, Numina would either go away, or they would stick around as something that isn't downstream from <i>any</i> of the gamelines. Path Magic would be completely disconnected from every gameline, including <b>Mage</b>; Psychic Phenomena would be their own thing, as would True Faith. (Though in the case of True Faith, I can see ways either to emulate it or to your it to a specific gameline: <b>Mage</b> could do something like True Faith through a mage who Practices Faith and has an Avatar that's willing and able to perform Magick on the mage's behalf, along the lines of Wild Talent; <b>Hunter</b> has a <b>Dark Ages: Inquisition</b> counterpart that explicitly uses <b>Hunter</b>-like systems to build a substitute for True Faith; <b>Mummy: the Resurrection</b>'s Balance is stated to be of the same broad nature as True Faith, which might allow a mortal who has both True Faith and a Merit granting access to Hekau to use their True Faith as of it was Balance any time Balance figures into how Hekau work. And finally, <b>Demon</b> assigns a Faith rating to mortals, though it operates in a completely different way than True Faith does.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-02T15:07:42-07:00","id":1562506,"title":"","content":"I like more diversity too, and I think Numina are a great way to give some more weight to the idea that the known splats aren't everything and mastering them isn't knowing all the weird things out there.<br /> <br /> One idea I just had is a type of Alchemy that can derive something from the splats by using important ingredients taken from them.<br /> <br /> True Faith is the one thing that may need some serious revision to be more broadly about faith based magic rather than just Christian Gothic tropes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-02T17:25:48-07:00","id":1562521,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1562506#post1562506\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">True Faith is the one thing that may need some serious revision to be more broadly about faith based magic rather than just Christian Gothic tropes.</div> </div> </div> </div>What else do you think should be covered by True Faith? Specifically.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-02T17:54:35-07:00","id":1562523,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1562521#post1562521\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What else do you think should be covered by True Faith? Specifically.</div> </div> </div> </div>More broad Faith-based effects that could represent other religions. Not their ritual magic, but the effects they put in the hands of laypeople.<br /> <br /> Small talismans for personal protection isn't a strictly Christian thing, but they don't necessarily work the same for every religion. At most they do in the sense of being seen in most as divine intervention.<br /> <br /> The cross against vampires thing is literature, but has roots in this use of the cross. True Faith codifies this as the faithful getting the protection they expect from mundane objects or actions because of how much they believe.<br /> <br /> I think it could be expanded to cover similar but not identical feats by people from other faiths. Protection against evil spirits, luck in some areas, finding lost objects or people... I'd rather discard True Faith than have it be exclusive for Christian stuff even if it doesn't say so, but I do think this space for lay faith could be better explored, while Hedge Magic and Sorcery cover more ritualistic, proper priestly magic (that also includes Christians).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-02T23:49:28-07:00","id":1562534,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1562523#post1562523\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">More broad Faith-based effects that could represent other religions. Not their ritual magic, but the effects they put in the hands of laypeople.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which other religions do you have in mind?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-03T00:44:18-07:00","id":1562537,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1562534#post1562534\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which other religions do you have in mind?</div> </div> </div> </div>Ahn... All of them? I don't get where you're trying to reach.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-03T05:49:37-07:00","id":1562547,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1562506#post1562506\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> True Faith is the one thing that may need some serious revision to be more broadly about faith based magic rather than just Christian Gothic tropes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe you can steal a few ideas from <b>Ars Magica. </b>Since that game was the inspiration for the whole W.of D. its takes on Faith based magic might be inspiring to you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-03T15:43:24-07:00","id":1562588,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1562537#post1562537\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ahn... All of them? I don't get where you're trying to reach.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm looking for an example; not a general \u201cother religions do it differently,\u201d but once specific case of another religion doing it differently. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-03T18:50:16-07:00","id":1562604,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1562588#post1562588\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm looking for an example; not a general \u201cother religions do it differently,\u201d but once specific case of another religion doing it differently.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, now I got it.<br /> <br /> Shinto has O-fuda and Kamidana. The O-fuda is a wooden plaque with the name of a deity that you can get in a temple. The Kamidana is a miniature shrine to place o-fuda. For the faithful the O-fuda is an archetypal presence of the deity, bringing their specific blessings to the house and its inhabitants. True Faith would have an effect that's centralized in the Kamidana of the Faithful rather than their person and effects depending on the deity chosen.<br /> <br /> Buddhism in Japan has omamori, small colorful cloth bags sewn with prayers inside. They bring luck or protection in particular areas, like traffic safety, education, prosperity in business, peace at home or protection of pregnancy. They can be used by the person who buy at the shrine, but are more commonly given as a gift. True Faith would protect the recipient rather than the Faithful and have a narrow but sustained effect.<br /> <br /> In Umbanda, initiation involves finding out your spirit patron. Amulets can be all kinds of things with material and color being the most important aspect. The patron is always around and know many things about people, being able to communicate in dreams and possess the faithful. True Faith would focus on powers to get information about people, their natures and secrets, and the presence of spiritual entities. When it activates (usually by the patron, not by the faithful choosing) it's a possession by an entity that can guide the Faithful or others, or harm people by telling uncomfortable truths and prophecies. Direct protection comes more for emotional or mental attacks, as the entity can use the possession to negate their effects.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-04T06:41:47-07:00","id":1562635,"title":"","content":"Out of curiosity, in how many of those traditions does the faith of the practitioner or the user have a significant impact on the results? I mean as opposed to other factors like physical care for the item, or spiritual status of the maker, etc?<br /> <br /> The idea of faith being a factor in driving away vampires and evil magics seems to be at least in part connected to the Christian distinction between Ex Opere Operato (by the work worked) and Ex Opere Operatis (by the work of the worker). Some things (mainly sacraments) just work if they are done properly, but many of the things that classically drive away evil, like holy water, crucifixes, etc., are sacramentals and therefore depend on the faith of the user. I don't know how foundational that is in other religions.<br /> <br /> Of course, True Faith has already gone fairly far afield from that distinction anyways, so I don't raise that as a barrier. I'm just curious if we'd be pushing it even farther or not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-04T07:41:49-07:00","id":1562642,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1562635#post1562635\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Out of curiosity, in how many of those traditions does the faith of the practitioner or the user have a significant impact on the results? I mean as opposed to other factors like physical care for the item, or spiritual status of the maker, etc?<br /> <br /> The idea of faith being a factor in driving away vampires and evil magics seems to be at least in part connected to the Christian distinction between Ex Opere Operato (by the work worked) and Ex Opere Operatis (by the work of the worker). Some things (mainly sacraments) just work if they are done properly, but many of the things that classically drive away evil, like holy water, crucifixes, etc., are sacramentals and therefore depend on the faith of the user. I don't know how foundational that is in other religions.<br /> <br /> Of course, True Faith has already gone fairly far afield from that distinction anyways, so I don't raise that as a barrier. I'm just curious if we'd be pushing it even farther or not.</div> </div> </div> </div>About the same, give or take levels of formalization.<br /> <br /> Christian Tradition to begin with isn't itself all that clear cut. Driving away evil in particular is less about physical threats than influence, so while using crosses to ward demonic influence is Ex Opere Operatis, the iconical vampire ward is at must Ex Opere Operato by whomever crafted the cross, and the object itself is what repels Dracula.<br /> <br /> So even when making it Ex Opere Operato, True Faith is stepping outside standard theology. It is also actually a blur by definition: the goal of True Faith isn't to reflect Ex Operate Operatis, as this is actually covered by faith based Paths of Sorcery/Hedge Magic, but to reflect things that should work as Ex Operate Operato or in a similar way, but the source of the work isn't an actual magical ritual, so the faith of the user is providing the otherwise absent power they expect to be there.<br /> <br /> Vampires should be driven away by crosses. If the artisan crafting the cross is a faith based Sorcerer, they can make this cross work. If a faith based Sorcerer blesses the cross, they can make this cross work. But if this is a cross without magical properties, True Faith makes it work because it should work just by being a correctly made cross and YHWH should make his presence felt.<br /> <br /> But strictly speaking, they all have some level of faith involved:<br /> <br /> Shinto is the less intensely so. Doing the work will bring the initial presence of the deity. But the ritual and care involved in maintenance are important to get their goodwill, and the deity is likely to leave if they feel you lacking, which will include lack of faith if it's noticeable (and the deity isn't watching you only at home).<br /> <br /> Buddhism is the opposite: everything requires Faith from the worker. All works and prayers are simultaneously empowered by the nature of the work, the blessings of the enlightened and the faith of the worker, the last one being the fundamental key. Nature and blessings can increase or decrease the power of faith, but if faith is lacking it's all in vain, while great faith will not only still have an effect, but attract the blessings and imbue the work with power, so much so that it lingers, and a great feat of faith establishes a new valid ritual in this process.<br /> <br /> Umbanda also puts a lot of weight in faith in a slightly different way. First because your faith is what opens the channel for possession. It's not the only way, but the orix\u00e1s aren't spiritual predators and won't force their way against your best interest, the reason why they don't possess others, only the faithful. Second because, as in Shinto and most other religions, they favor those who either believe or are fated to believe, but by their nature they just <i><b>know</b></i> how much faith you have, as knowing the heart of people is their whole thing. In fact, they would be able to tell the faith of anyone in anything, and they respect other religions if the teachings are positive (a trait derived from its links to Kardecism).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-06T15:12:13-07:00","id":1562797,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matter911</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms#post1548401\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Something I've wondered, but can't seem to find a definite answer, but how do mages with opposing Paradigms view/rationalize each other's magick. (ex. Etherite and Verbena, or Tradition and Technocracy). Is it that they attempt to rationalize what other mages do under their worldview (&quot;Oh it's not the blood sacrifice that makes it work, it's the application of Ether blah blah blah...&quot;) or do the mages understand that belief is what fuels their magic, and therefore no one is entirely 'wrong' in their practice. Or is neither correct and I'm missing something?</div> </div> </div> </div>Returning to this thread, I had it as this:<br /> <br /> &quot;A hedge mage thinks that their magic is empowered by Hermeticism and angels. A true mage knows it's because he believes in Hermeticism and angels. The truly enlightened knows he is Hermeticism and Angels.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\u2615","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-06T19:45:15-07:00","id":1562809,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1562797#post1562797\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Returning to this thread, I had it as this:<br /> <br /> &quot;A hedge mage thinks that their magic is empowered by Hermeticism and angels. A true mage knows it's because he believes in Hermeticism and angels. The truly enlightened knows he is Hermeticism and Angels.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>But that's not true. They may all believe any of the three. In fact, depending on the Hedge Mage, their system can teach any of them. A lot of modern Occultism goes by the third.<br /> <br /> Instead, going by the rules:<br /> <br /> The Hedge Mage's magic comes from knowing rituals that actually work. How this came to be is another can of worms, but the ritual in fact does what it does because the ritual has magic.<br /> <br /> A Sorcerer seems to have a connection to the Umbrae that affects their magic in some way. Still, the ritual still has something to it, and whatever makes the Sorcerer special, it has nothing to do with belief: their special drive is a fact regardless of what they think.<br /> <br /> Mages are super special and bend reality to their beliefs/will. Their Magic is the only one that require belief in some sense. And the only for which belief suffices. They don't need ritual that work by themselves.<br /> <br /> What a true enlightened believes? No one knows. That's the great question of Ascension, and up for each one to find out. But here's a hint: raising Arete and shedding Instruments has no effect in it.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-07T05:06:31-07:00","id":1562825,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1562809#post1562809\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> But that's not true. They may all believe any of the three. In fact, depending on the Hedge Mage, their system can teach any of them. A lot of modern Occultism goes by the third.<br /> <br /> Instead, going by the rules:<br /> <br /> The Hedge Mage's magic comes from knowing rituals that actually work. How this came to be is another can of worms, but the ritual in fact does what it does because the ritual has magic.<br /> <br /> A Sorcerer seems to have a connection to the Umbrae that affects their magic in some way. Still, the ritual still has something to it, and whatever makes the Sorcerer special, it has nothing to do with belief: their special drive is a fact regardless of what they think.<br /> <br /> Mages are super special and bend reality to their beliefs/will. Their Magic is the only one that require belief in some sense. And the only for which belief suffices. They don't need ritual that work by themselves.<br /> <br /> What a true enlightened believes? No one knows. That's the great question of Ascension, and up for each one to find out. But here's a hint: raising Arete and shedding Instruments has no effect in it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I feel there's no consistency whatsoever in terms of the philosophy of mages. They made changes so Paradigm became a trap you weren't supposed to overcome and thus the gameline's philosophy sort of fell apart.<br /> <br /> Mind you, &quot;Consensual Reality&quot; was an idea that was so problematic I understand why they did it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-07T06:28:12-07:00","id":1562828,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1562825#post1562825\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yeah, I feel there's no consistency whatsoever in terms of the philosophy of mages. They made changes so Paradigm became a trap you weren't supposed to overcome and thus the gameline's philosophy sort of fell apart.<br /> <br /> Mind you, &quot;Consensual Reality&quot; was an idea that was so problematic I understand why they did it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that falling apart is more a you problem than a gameline problem. Except by the fact this came is fucking dense to understand, which is not a good thing by itself, rather is more of a price.<br /> <br /> For starters, there's no unified philosophy among Mages, and that's by design. What is actually happening is objectively supposed to be unknown. Rules are real, but <i>explanations</i> about Spheres, Arete, Avatars, Paradox, Quint, Consensus and everything else are just theory, those things may even not exist, just look like it as byproducts of phenomena Traditions and Technocracy are unaware of. Mage may not be what they think at all, or even the same thing, but rather a collection of things that can be modeled by the same mechanics and look like they're dealing with similar phenomenon out of random happenstance or some convergence factor.<br /> <br /> Paradigm isn't a trap, it is a lens. You can overcome it, in fact there are several ways of doing so, though most Mages have no reason to want that. I don't know from where you took that. From a character perspective doing so is kinda hard, but for the player some options are quite simple.<br /> <br /> But keeping to the baseline, what you missed is that even from 1st Ed, Mage isn't talking about <i>every</i> magic. They're talking about Mage Magic or Magick, the &quot;pedantic K&quot; has a reason to be there.<br /> <br /> Mages, Sorcerers and Hedge Magicians aren't at different levels of doing the same thing. They are doing <b><i>completely different things</i></b>. At least as far as anyone can tell (conciliating is possible, but that's the intended ambiguity of the setting, not how it is meant to be in simple games). And the Traditions and Technocracy know that (among Crafts that varies).<br /> <br /> And that's why your previous comparison was wrong. Not because of what they think they do, but because they never did the same thing and the answer is objectively different for each. You can think about this as different wolf shifters:<br /> <br /> A Garou, a Fomor, a Protean user, a Vicissitude user, a Mage with Life, a Changeling with Fae and Naming and a Demon with the right Lore and/or Apocalyptic Form can all shape change into wolves, giant hybrids or interstitial forms. But each is doing a fundamentally different things, and what one believes about what they do, right or wrong, has nothing to do with what the others do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-10T12:16:15-07:00","id":1562970,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1548401-how-do-the-traditions-view-opposing-paradigms?p=1562828#post1562828\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For starters, there's no unified philosophy among Mages, and that's by design. What is actually happening is objectively supposed to be unknown. Rules are real, but <i>explanations</i> about Spheres, Arete, Avatars, Paradox, Quint, Consensus and everything else are just theory, those things may even not exist, just look like it as byproducts of phenomena Traditions and Technocracy are unaware of. Mage may not be what they think at all, or even the same thing, but rather a collection of things that can be modeled by the same mechanics and look like they're dealing with similar phenomenon out of random happenstance or some convergence factor.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can't remember who made it, but I saw a video a while back about a newly awakened mage, that I feel made a very good point about Traditionalist magic.<br /> <br /> Long story short, they specifically pointed out that there were different iterations of witchcraft, and how not everyone uses white sage in their rituals.<br /> <br /> They did this while they were in character, by the way, as the MC of the story was running an herbal magic shop.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}